<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sibghatullah Mojaddedi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1925">1925</a>–<a href="/years/2019">2019</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sibghatullah Mojaddedi was an Afghan religious scholar from a leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> family and leader of a moderate mujahideen party during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-afghan-war">Soviet–Afghan War</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a> Accord he became the first president of the Islamic State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> in April 1992, serving for two months before handing over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burhanuddin-rabbani">Burhanuddin Rabbani</a>. He later chaired the loya jirga that adopted Afghanistan's 2004 constitution.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sibghatullah-mojaddedi.jpg" alt="Sibghatullah Mojaddedi" width="320" height="424" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: U.S. State Department · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mojaddedi_in_September_2014_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sibghatullah Mojaddedi was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, into the Mojaddedis, a well-known Pashtun family of religious scholars who trace their ancestry to the 16th-century scholar and Naqshbandi Sufi Mujaddid Ahmad Sirhindi. He studied Islamic law at al-Azhar University in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> in 1952 to teach in high schools and at Kabul University, where he became known as an advocate of Afghan political independence. In 1959 he was accused of conspiring against the Soviet prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikita-khrushchev">Nikita Khrushchev</a> and was imprisoned without trial until 1964. After his release he was forced into exile for outspoken comments on Soviet influence, living in several countries including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saur-revolution">Saur Revolution</a> of 1978 the communist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalq">Khalq</a> government killed Mojaddedi's brother and several relatives. From exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a> he founded the Jebh-e-Nejat-e Melli, the Afghan National Liberation Front, and on 13 March 1979 was the first person to call for nationwide <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> against the Democratic Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. His militia was most prominent in Kunar Province. He envisioned an Islamic republic, possibly with a restored monarchy. In 1988 he was elected head of the Afghan Interim Government in Peshawar.</p><p>In April 1992 he was elected chair of the Islamic Jihad Council set up to form a post-Soviet government, and on 28 April he entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> amid a large crowd. He offered a general amnesty to all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> except the deposed president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-najibullah">Mohammad Najibullah</a>. All mujahideen factions supported his election except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hezb-e-islami">Hezb-e Islami Gulbuddin</a>, whose forces fired rockets at the capital. In May 1992 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burhanuddin-rabbani">Burhanuddin Rabbani</a> set up a new leadership council that undermined his authority, and Mojaddedi resigned and handed over power. Sources differ on whether his time in office lasted two or three months.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the fall of the Taliban in 2001 Mojaddedi returned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> and chaired the loya jirga of 2003 that approved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>'s new constitution. Amnesty International said that he and the jirga's leadership curtailed freedom of speech there. In 2005 he became chairman of the Meshrano Jirga, the upper house of the National Assembly. On 12 March 2006 two suicide bombers attacked his car in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, killing four pedestrians and slightly injuring him. He died on 11 February 2019. He is considered to have been a moderate leader.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sibghatullah Mojaddedi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Sibghatullah Mojaddedi</b> served briefly as president, followed by Burhanuddin Rabbani of the Jamiat-e Islami.</blockquote><a href="/state/islamic-state-of-afghanistan">Islamic State of Afghanistan</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.53,69.17&amp;year=1986" aria-label="Kabul on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6307 913 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sibghatullah Mojaddedi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6757 1183h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6757 1183h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.53,69.17&amp;year=1986">What was here in 1986? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
